[E4a7de7] {"title":"Honey bee","text":"Honey bee
Only one fossil species is documented from the New World , Apis nearctica , known from a single 14 million - year - old specimen from Nevada . The close relatives of modern honey bees -- e.g. bumblebees and stingless bees -- are also social to some degree , and social behavior seems a plesiomorphic trait that predates the origin of the genus . Among the extant members of Apis , the more basal species make single , exposed combs , while the more recently evolved species nest in cavities and have multiple combs , which has greatly facilitated their domestication . Most species have historically been cultured or at least exploited for honey and beeswax by humans indigenous to their native ranges . Only two species have been truly domesticated : Apis mellifera and Apis cerana indica . A. mellifera has been cultivated at least since the time of the building of the Egyptian pyramids , and only that species has been moved extensively beyond its native range . Honey bees are the only extant members of the tribe Apini .","entities":["Honey bees","Apis nearctica","Stingless bees","Social behavior","Apis mellifera","Apis cerana indica","Building of the Egyptian pyramids","Apis","Apini","Bumblebees","New World","Nevada"],"facts":["Honey bees -RELATED_TO-> honey","Darwin -RELATED_TO-> Honey bees","Honey bees -RELATED_TO-> hexagonal cells","A History of Honey Bees in the Hawaiian Islands -RELATED_TO-> Honey bees","Apis florea -PART_OF-> Honey bees"]}
