The genetics of the western honey bee A. mellifera are unclear . The domesticated species are A. cerana indica and A. mellifera Borneo honey bee ( edit ) The reddish Koschevnikov 's bee ( Apis koschevnikovi ) from Borneo is clearly a distinct species ; it probably derives from the first colonization of the island by cave - nesting honey bees . Eastern honey bee ( edit ) Apis cerana , the eastern honey bee proper , is the traditional honey bee of southern and eastern Asia . It was domesticated as subspecies A. c. indica and kept in hives in a fashion similar to A. mellifera , though on a more limited , regional scale . It has not been possible yet to resolve its relationship to the Bornean A. c. nuluensis and Apis nigrocincta from the Philippines to satisfaction ; the most recent hypothesis is that these are indeed distinct species , but that A. cerana is still paraphyletic , consisting of several separate species . Western honey bee ( edit ) The European honey bee may have originated from eastern Africa . This bee is pictured in Tanzania .","entities":["Philippines","Western honey bee","Eastern honey bee","Koschevnikov's bee","A. c. nuluensis","Southern Asia","Eastern Asia","cave-nesting honey bees","A. cerana indica","Apis nigrocincta","eastern Africa","Tanzania","Borneo"],"facts":["Philippines -LOCATED_IN-> Jeepneys","Philippines -LOCATED_IN-> MD Juan","Philippines -LOCATED_IN-> E-jeepneys","Philippines -LOCATED_IN-> LSV","Philippines -LOCATED_IN-> Willys MB"]}

