Lateral parts ( edit ) The lateral parts of the middle fossa are of considerable depth , and support the temporal lobes of the brain . They are marked by depressions for the brain convolutions and traversed by furrows for the anterior and posterior branches of the middle meningeal vessels . These furrows begin near the foramen spinosum , and the anterior runs forward and upward to the sphenoidal angle of the parietal , where it is sometimes converted into a bony canal ; the posterior runs lateralward and backward across the temporal squama and passes on to the parietal near the middle of its lower border . The following apertures are also to be seen . In front is the superior orbital fissure , bounded above by the small wing , below , by the great wing , and medially , by the body of the sphenoid ; it is usually completed laterally by the orbital plate of the frontal bone .","entities":["Middle cranial fossa","temporal lobes","Lateral parts of the middle fossa","Depressions","Brain convolutions","Anterior furrow","Posterior furrow","Anterior branch of the middle meningeal vessels","Posterior branch of the middle meningeal vessels","Middle meningeal vessels","Sphenoidal angle of the parietal","Parietal","Bony canal","Temporal squama","Superior orbital fissure"],"facts":["Middle cranial fossa -RELATED_TO-> posterior fossa","Middle cranial fossa -RELATED_TO-> clivus","Middle cranial fossa -RELATED_TO-> petrous crest","Middle cranial fossa -RELATED_TO-> lesser wings of the sphenoid bone","Middle cranial fossa -RELATED_TO-> anterior cranial fossa"]}
