[Ec36822] {"title":"Economy of India","text":"Economy of India
Ancient and medieval eras The citizens of the Indus Valley Civilisation , a permanent settlement that flourished between 2800 BC and 1800 BC , practised agriculture , domesticated animals , used uniform weights and measures , made tools and weapons , and traded with other cities . Evidence of well - planned streets , a drainage system and water supply reveals their knowledge of urban planning , which included the first - known urban sanitation systems and the existence of a form of municipal government . The spice trade between India and Europe was the main catalyst for the Age of Discovery . For a continuous duration of nearly 1700 years from the year 1 AD , India is the top most economy constituting 35 to 40 % of world GDP . Maritime trade was carried out extensively between South India and Southeast and West Asia from early times until around the fourteenth century AD . Both the Malabar and Coromandel Coasts were the sites of important trading centres from as early as the first century BC , used for import and export as well as transit points between the Mediterranean region and southeast Asia . Over time , traders organised themselves into associations which received state patronage .","entities":["India","Coromandel Coast","Mediterranean region","West Asia","southeast Asia","Indus Valley Civilisation","urban planning","urban sanitation systems","municipal government","spice trade","Age of Discovery","maritime trade","South India","Malabar Coast","trading centres"],"facts":["India -PART_OF-> Beekeeping by countries","India -PART_OF-> British Empire","India -PART_OF-> these ten countries","India -PART_OF-> Gondwana","India -PART_OF-> National anthems of Asia"]}

