Internal Revenue Code , ranging in severity which is reflected in the amount of the applicable fines . Contents ( hide ) 1 Underestimate and late payment penalties 2 Penalties for failure to timely file returns or timely pay tax 3 Accuracy related penalties 4 Penalties in connection with information returns 5 100 % penalty on unpaid withholding taxes 6 Penalties for failure to provide foreign information 7 Excise taxes as penalties 8 Tax fraud penalties 9 Tax adviser penalties 10 Judicial appeal of penalties 11 Further reading 12 References Underestimate and late payment penalties ( edit ) Taxpayers are required to have withholding of tax or make quarterly estimated tax payments before the end of the tax year . Because accurate estimation requires accurate prediction of the future , taxpayers sometimes underestimate the amount due . The penalty for paying too little estimated tax or having too little tax withheld is computed with interest on the amount that should have been , but was not , paid . Where a taxpayer has filed an income or excise tax return that shows a balance due but does not pay that balance by the due date of the return ( without extensions ) , a different charge applies .","entities":["IRS penalties","Taxpayers","Withholding of tax","Quarterly estimated tax payments","Tax year","Penalty for paying too little estimated tax or having too little tax withheld","Amount that should have been paid","Income tax return","Excise tax return","Balance due","Due date of the return","IRS","interest","Internal Revenue Code"],"facts":["IRS penalties -RELATED_TO-> forfeiture of property","IRS penalties -RELATED_TO-> criminal penalties","IRS penalties -RELATED_TO-> excise taxes","IRS penalties -RELATED_TO-> income tax returns","IRS penalties -RELATED_TO-> monetary penalties"]}
