Best alternative to a negotiated agreement - wikipedia Best alternative to a negotiated agreement Jump to : navigation , search This article needs additional citations for verification . Please help improve this article by adding citations to reliable sources . Unsourced material may be challenged and removed . ( July 2013 ) ( Learn how and when to remove this template message ) In negotiation theory , the best alternative to a negotiated agreement or BATNA is the most advantageous alternative course of action a party can take if negotiations fail and an agreement can not be reached . BATNA is the key focus and the driving force behind a successful negotiator . A party should generally not accept a worse resolution than its BATNA . Care should be taken , however , to ensure that deals are accurately valued , taking into account all considerations , such as relationship value , time value of money and the likelihood that the other party will live up to their side of the bargain . These other considerations are often difficult to value , since they are frequently based on uncertain or qualitative considerations , rather than easily measurable and quantifiable factors .","entities":["Best alternative to a negotiated agreement","negotiation theory","alternative course of action","negotiations","agreement","negotiator","relationship value","time value of money","uncertain or qualitative considerations","measurable and quantifiable factors","resolution","party"],"facts":["Best alternative to a negotiated agreement -RELATED_TO-> Conflict resolution research","Best alternative to a negotiated agreement -RELATED_TO-> Negotiation Experts","Best alternative to a negotiated agreement -RELATED_TO-> Negotiation","Best alternative to a negotiated agreement -RELATED_TO-> disagreement point","Best alternative to a negotiated agreement -RELATED_TO-> YArooms"]}
[Eae134e] {"title":"Best alternative to a negotiated agreement","text":"Best alternative to a negotiated agreement
History ( edit ) BATNA was developed by negotiation researchers Roger Fisher and William Ury of the Harvard Program on Negotiation ( PON ) , in their series of books on Principled negotiation that started with Getting to YES , unwittingly duplicating the game theory concept of a disagreement point from bargaining problems pioneered by Nobel Laureate John Forbes Nash decades earlier . A Nash Equilibrium is reached among a group of players when no player can benefit from changing strategies if every other player sticks to their current strategy . For example , Amy and Phil are in Nash Equilibrium if Amy is making the best decision she can , taking into account Phil 's decision , and Phil is making the best decision he can , taking into account Amy 's decision . Likewise , a group of players are in Nash Equilibrium if each one is making the best decision that he or she can , taking into account the decisions of the others .","entities":["Best alternative to a negotiated agreement","Roger Fisher","William Ury","Harvard Program on Negotiation","Principled negotiation","disagreement point","bargaining problems","John Forbes Nash","Nash Equilibrium","Getting to YES","Amy","Phil","Game theory"],"facts":["Best alternative to a negotiated agreement -RELATED_TO-> alternative course of action","Best alternative to a negotiated agreement -RELATED_TO-> negotiations","Best alternative to a negotiated agreement -RELATED_TO-> agreement","Best alternative to a negotiated agreement -RELATED_TO-> negotiator","Best alternative to a negotiated agreement -RELATED_TO-> negotiation theory"]}
