Until the Courts Act 2003 came into force , magistrates were tied to a particular area ( see magistrates ' courts committee , commission area , petty sessions area ) . This has now been changed such that they are assigned to local justice areas , but less strongly . The Courts Act provides the current framework for appointment of the justices , which is done by the Lord Chancellor in the name of sovereign . Justices can also be removed by the same mechanism . District judges ( magistrates ' court ) -- previously known as stipendiary magistrates -- must have a seven years ' general legal qualification , and are appointed by the sovereign on the advice of the Lord Chancellor . Before 1714 , magistrates were liable to be approached at any time and in any place by people legally recognised as paupers , appealing for aid if parish authorities refused to provide any . It was relatively common for these magistrates to write out , on the spot , an order requiring aid to be granted . Scotland ( edit ) See also : Judiciary of Scotland Within the Scottish legal system justices of the peace are lay magistrates who currently sit in the justice of the peace courts .","entities":["Scotland","magistrates","Courts Act 2003","particular area","local justice areas","Lord Chancellor","sovereign","District judges (magistrates' court)","seven years' general legal qualification","Judiciary of Scotland","parish authorities","people legally recognised as paupers","Justice of the peace courts","Justices of the peace"],"facts":["Scotland -LOCATED_IN-> UK","Scotland -LOCATED_IN-> Europe","James -LOCATED_IN-> Scotland","1997 referendums in Scotland and Wales -LOCATED_IN-> Scotland","Scotland -LOCATED_IN-> United Kingdom"]}
