Pharmacological control over pupil size remains an important part of the treatment of some ocular diseases . Drugs can also reduce the metabolically active process of secreting aqueous humour , which is important in treating both acute and chronic glaucoma . Immunology ( edit ) The normal uvea consists of immune competent cells , particularly lymphocytes , and is prone to respond to inflammation by developing lymphocytic infiltrates . A rare disease called sympathetic ophthalmia may represent ' cross-reaction ' between the uveal and retinal antigens ( i.e. , the body 's inability to distinguish between them , with resulting misdirected inflammatory reactions ) . Clinical significance ( edit ) See uveitis , choroiditis , iritis , iridocyclitis , anterior uveitis , sympathetic ophthalmia , and uveal melanoma . References ( edit ) Jump up ^ https://en.oxforddictionaries.com/definition/uvea Jump up ^ eye , human. `` Encyclopædia Britannica from Encyclopædia Britannica 2006 Ultimate Reference Suite DVD 2009 External links ( edit ) MedlinePlus Encyclopedia 002337 Diagram at visionweb.","entities":["inflammation","Uvea","Pharmacological control over pupil size","Pupil size","Ocular diseases","Aqueous humour","Glaucoma","Immune competent cells","Lymphocytes","Lymphocytic infiltrates","Sympathetic ophthalmia","Cross-reaction","Uveal antigens","Retinal antigens","Uveitis"],"facts":["inflammation -RELATED_TO-> biliary tree","inflammation -RELATED_TO-> liver","Liver function tests -RELATED_TO-> inflammation","white cell count -RELATED_TO-> inflammation","inflammation -RELATED_TO-> Gallbladder"]}
