1 Common treatment modalities 3.2. 2 Uncommon treatment modalities 4 Notable researchers and discoveries 5 Additional images 6 See also 7 References 8 Further reading 9 External links Structure ( edit ) Inverted versus non-inverted retina ( edit ) The vertebrate retina is inverted in the sense that the light sensing cells are in back of the retina , so that light has to pass through layers of neurons and capillaries before it reaches the rods and cones . In contrast , in the cephalopod retina the photoreceptors are in front , with processing neurons and capillaries behind them . Because of this , cephalopods do not have a blind spot . The overlying neural fibre layer does not degrade vision in the inverted retina . The neurons are transparent and the accompanying glial cells have been shown to act as fibre - optic channels to transport photons directly to the photoreceptors . In any case , the fovea centralis , the part of the retina with the highest resolution , does not have a layer of blood vessels and nerve cells over it , so there is no possibility of image degradation here .","entities":["vision","Blind spot","vertebrate retina","cephalopod retina","light sensing cells","processing neurons","neural fibre layer","fibre-optic channels","fovea centralis","blood vessels","image degradation","rods","capillaries","cones","photoreceptors"],"facts":["vision -RELATED_TO-> biochemistry","vision -RELATED_TO-> animal eyes","vision -RELATED_TO-> Animals","vision -RELATED_TO-> higher vertebrates","vision -RELATED_TO-> Aurora"]}

