Starting in the grasslands of the rift , Homo erectus , the predecessor of modern humans , found an ecological niche as a tool - maker and developed a dependence on it , becoming a `` tool equipped savanna dweller . '' Stone Age in archaeology Beginning of the Stone Age Obsidian projectile point The oldest indirect evidence found of stone tool use is fossilised animal bones with tool marks ; these are 3.4 million years old and were found in the Lower Awash Valley in Ethiopia . Archaeological discoveries in Kenya in 2015 , identifying possibly the oldest known evidence of hominin use of tools to date , have indicated that Kenyanthropus platyops ( a 3.2 to 3.5 - million - year - old Pliocene hominin fossil discovered in Lake Turkana , Kenya in 1999 ) may have been the earliest tool - users known . The oldest stone tools were excavated from the site of Lomekwi 3 in West Turkana , northwestern Kenya , and date to 3.3 million years old .","entities":["Ethiopia","Kenya","Pliocene","modern humans","Obsidian projectile point","stone tool use","fossilised animal bones","Lower Awash Valley","Archaeological discoveries in Kenya","Kenyanthropus platyops","Lake Turkana","archaeology","Lomekwi 3","Homo erectus","stone tools"],"facts":["Ethiopia -LOCATED_IN-> Sub-Saharan Africa","Ethiopia -LOCATED_IN-> Africa","Al-Habash -LOCATED_IN-> Ethiopia","East Sudanese savanna -LOCATED_IN-> Ethiopia","Ethiopia -LOCATED_IN-> East"]}
