The 3 / 8 '' and 5 / 8 '' needles are most commonly used . Usually , no more than 1 mL of solution is given , compared to intradermal injections , where no more than 0.5 mL is usually given . Procedure ( edit ) Subcutaneous injections are inserted at 45 to 90 degree angles , depending on amount of subcutaneous tissue present and length of needle - a shorter , 3 / 8 '' needle is usually inserted 90 degrees and a 5 / 8 '' needle is usually inserted at 45 degrees . Medication is administered slowly , about 10 seconds / milliliter . Subcutaneous insulin injections in diabetes mellitus ( edit ) An insulin pump with a subcutaneous injection site . A person with insulin - dependent ( either type 1 or type 2 ) diabetes mellitus typically injects insulin subcutaneously . The injection should be given under the skin , into the fat layer .","entities":["Subcutaneous injection","Intradermal injections","Subcutaneous tissue","Medication","Insulin","Diabetes mellitus","Type 1 diabetes mellitus","Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Insulin pump","Subcutaneous injection site","Fat layer","Skin"],"facts":["Subcutaneous injection -RELATED_TO-> Medications","Subcutaneous injection -RELATED_TO-> Subcutaneous administration","Subcutaneous injection -RELATED_TO-> Subcutis","Subcutaneous injection -RELATED_TO-> Vaccines","Subcutaneous injection -RELATED_TO-> Intravenous injection"]}
