It is slower than intramuscular injections but still faster than intradermal injections . Contents ( hide ) 1 Injection sites 2 Equipment 3 Procedure 4 Subcutaneous insulin injections in diabetes mellitus 5 See also 6 References Injection sites ( edit ) Subcutaneous injection sites Sites include , The outer area of the upper arm . The abdomen , from the rib margin to the iliac crest and avoiding a 2 - inch circle around the navel . This has the fastest rate of absorption among the sites . The front of the thigh , midway to the outer side , 4 inches below the top of the thigh to 4 inches above the knee . This has a slower rate of absorption than the upper arm . The upper back . The upper area of the buttock , just behind the hip bone . This has the slowest rate of absorption among the sites . Equipment ( edit ) A 25 to 31 gauge thick , 3 / 8 '' to 1 '' long needle can be used . The size is determined by the amount of subcutaneous tissue present , which is based on patient build .","entities":["Subcutaneous injection","Intramuscular injections","Intradermal injections","Outer area of the upper arm","Abdomen","Front of the thigh","Upper back","Upper area of the buttock","Needle","Subcutaneous tissue","Patient build","Subcutaneous insulin injections","Diabetes mellitus"],"facts":["Subcutaneous injection -LOCATED_IN-> Skin","Subcutaneous injection -RELATED_TO-> Subcutaneous administration","Subcutaneous injection -RELATED_TO-> Subcutis","Subcutaneous injection -RELATED_TO-> Vaccines","Subcutaneous injection -LOCATED_IN-> Fat layer"]}
