Although a Vitis vinifera variety , it is a grape of `` very modest '' quality . Jean - Louis Vignes was one of the early settlers to use a higher quality vinifera in his vineyard near Los Angeles . The first winery in the United States to become commercially successful was founded in Cincinnati , Ohio in the mid-1830s by Nicholas Longworth . He made a sparkling wine from Catawba grapes . By 1855 Ohio had 1500 acres in vineyards , according to travel writer Frederick Law Olmsted , who said it was more than in Missouri and Illinois , which each had 1100 acres in wine . German immigrants from the late 1840s had been instrumental in building the wine industry in those states . In the 1860s , vineyards in the Ohio River Valley were attacked by Black rot . This prompted several winemakers to move north to the Finger Lakes region of western New York . During this time , the Missouri wine industry , centered on the German colony in Hermann , was expanding rapidly along both shores of the Missouri River west of St. Louis .","entities":["Missouri","St. Louis","United States","Ohio","Missouri River","Los Angeles","Vitis vinifera","Jean-Louis Vignes","first winery in the United States to become commercially successful","Nicholas Longworth","sparkling wine","Catawba grapes","Frederick Law Olmsted","German immigrants","wine industry"],"facts":["Missouri -LOCATED_IN-> US","Missouri -LOCATED_IN-> Midwestern United States","Missouri -LOCATED_IN-> U.S.","Kansas City -LOCATED_IN-> Missouri","Warsaw, Missouri -LOCATED_IN-> Missouri"]}
