Vanishing point - wikipedia Vanishing point Jump to : navigation , search For other uses , see Vanishing point ( disambiguation ) . A vanishing point can be seen at the far end of this railroad . A vanishing point is a point on the image plane of a perspective drawing where the two - dimensional perspective projections ( or drawings ) of mutually parallel lines in three - dimensional space appear to converge . When the set of parallel lines is perpendicular to a picture plane , the construction is known as one - point perspective , and their vanishing point corresponds to the oculus , or `` eye point '' , from which the image should be viewed for correct perspective geometry . Traditional linear drawings use objects with one to three sets of parallels , defining one to three vanishing points . Part of a series on Graphical projection Planar ( show ) Parallel projection Orthographic projection Multiview projection Axonometric projection Isometric projection Oblique projection Perspective projection Curvilinear perspective Reverse perspective Views ( show ) 2.","entities":["image","perspective drawing","perspective projections","mutually parallel lines","three-dimensional space","one-point perspective","picture plane","oculus","perspective geometry","traditional linear drawings","Graphical projection","Parallel projection","Orthographic projection","Multiview projection","Axonometric projection"],"facts":["image -RELATED_TO-> Retina","focal length -RELATED_TO-> image","subtractive drawing -RELATED_TO-> image","refractive index -RELATED_TO-> image","vanishing point theorem -RELATED_TO-> picture plane"]}
