Using this definition , it is easy to show that if X and Y are random variables and Y is constant , then X and Y are independent , since the σ - algebra generated by a constant random variable is the trivial σ - algebra ( ∅ , Ω ) . Probability zero events can not affect independence so independence also holds if Y is only Pr - almost surely constant . Properties ( edit ) Self - independence ( edit ) Note that an event is independent of itself if and only if P ( A ) = P ( A ∩ A ) = P ( A ) ⋅ P ( A ) ⇔ P ( A ) = 0 or 1 ( \ displaystyle \ mathrm ( P ) ( A ) = \ mathrm ( P ) ( A \ cap A ) = \ mathrm ( P ) ( A ) \ cdot \ mathrm ( P ) ( A ) \ Leftrightarrow \ mathrm ( P ) ( A ) = 0 ( \ text ( or ) ) 1 ) . Thus an event is independent of itself if and only if it almost surely occurs or its complement almost surely occurs .","entities":["Probability","Probability theory","Self-independence","X","Y","constant random variable","σ-algebra","trivial σ-algebra","probability zero events","event","complement","Independence","random variables"],"facts":["Probability -RELATED_TO-> Range of possible values","continuous probability distribution -RELATED_TO-> Probability","credit quality -RELATED_TO-> Probability","Probability -PART_OF-> conditional probability","Independence (probability theory) -RELATED_TO-> Probability theory"]}

