Unsourced material may be challenged and removed . ( December 2016 ) ( Learn how and when to remove this template message ) In 1963 , the musical team Peter , Paul and Mary , along with their musical director Milt Okun , adapted and rewrote `` Go Tell It on the Mountain '' as `` Tell It on the Mountain '' , their lyrics referring specifically to : Exodus and using the phrase `` Let my people go , '' but referring implicitly to the Civil Rights struggle of the early 1960s . This version became a moderately successful single for them ( US # 33 pop , 1964 ) . According to Religious Studies professor and Civil Rights historian Charles Marsh , it was African American Civil Rights leader Fannie Lou Hamer who combined this song with the spiritual `` Go Down Moses , '' taking the last line of the chorus , `` Let my people go '' and substituting it in the chorus of `` Go Tell It on the Mountain . ''","entities":["Peter, Paul and Mary","Milt Okun","Tell It on the Mountain","Exodus","Civil Rights struggle of the early 1960s","Go Down Moses","Fannie Lou Hamer","Charles Marsh","Let my people go","Go Tell It on the Mountain"],"facts":["Go Tell It on the Mountain -RELATED_TO-> English","Go Tell It on the Mountain -RELATED_TO-> Christmas","Peter, Paul and Mary -CREATED-> eponymous debut album","Peter, Paul and Mary -RELATED_TO-> Where Have All the Flowers Gone?","Go Tell It on the Mountain -RELATED_TO-> spiritual"]}
