[E861c58] {"title":"Humus","text":"Humus
Plant remains , including those that animals digested and excreted , contain organic compounds : sugars , starches , proteins , carbohydrates , lignins , waxes , resins , and organic acids . Decay in the soil begins with the decomposition of sugars and starches from carbohydrates , which decompose easily as detritivores initially invade the dead plant organs , while the remaining cellulose and lignin decompose more slowly . Simple proteins , organic acids , starches , and sugars decompose rapidly , while crude proteins , fats , waxes , and resins remain relatively unchanged for longer periods of time . Lignin , which is quickly transformed by white - rot fungi , is one of the primary precursors of humus , together with by - products of microbial and animal activity . The humus produced by humification is thus a mixture of compounds and complex biological chemicals of plant , animal , or microbial origin that has many functions and benefits in soil . Some judge earthworm humus ( vermicompost ) to be the optimal organic manure .","entities":["cellulose","Proteins","carbohydrates","Humus","Soil","humification","Plant remains","Organic compounds","Sugars","Lignin","Waxes","Resins","Organic acids","Detritivores","White-rot fungi"],"facts":["Hexose phosphates -RELATED_TO-> cellulose","Hops -CONTAINS-> cellulose","Proteins -RELATED_TO-> vaginal fluids","Proteins -RELATED_TO-> Digestion","Proteins -RELATED_TO-> major groove"]}
