Contents ( hide ) 1 History 2 Neuromuscular blockers 3 Spasmolytics 3.1 Terminology 3.2 Clinical use 3.3 Mechanism 4 Side effects 5 See also 6 References 7 External links History ( edit ) The earliest known use of muscle relaxant drugs was by natives of the Amazon Basin in South America who used poison - tipped arrows that produced death by skeletal muscle paralysis . This was first documented in the 16th century , when European explorers encountered it . This poison , known today as curare , led to some of the earliest scientific studies in pharmacology . Its active ingredient , tubocurarine , as well as many synthetic derivatives , played a significant role in scientific experiments to determine the function of acetylcholine in neuromuscular transmission . By 1943 , neuromuscular blocking drugs became established as muscle relaxants in the practice of anesthesia and surgery . The U.S. Food and Drug Administration ( FDA ) approved the use of carisoprodol in 1959 , metaxalone in August 1962 , and cyclobenzaprine in August 1977 .","entities":["Pharmacology","South America","Amazon Basin","natives of the Amazon Basin","poison-tipped arrows","skeletal muscle paralysis","European explorers","curare","documentation of curare use","tubocurarine","synthetic derivatives","neuromuscular transmission","scientific experiments","neuromuscular blocking drugs","anesthesia"],"facts":["Neurophysiologists -RELATED_TO-> Pharmacology","Pharmacology -RELATED_TO-> Pupil","major drug groups -PART_OF-> Pharmacology","South America -LOCATED_IN-> plains","South America -LOCATED_IN-> mountains"]}
