The musician 's son , Jay Perry , took the opportunity to have his father 's body re-examined to verify the original findings , and asked forensic anthropologist William Bass to carry out the procedure . Among the rumors surrounding the accident that this second examination sought to verify was that an accidental firearm discharge took place on board the aircraft and caused the crash , since two months after the event , a farmer had found at the crash site a . 22 ( 5.6 mm ) pistol known to have belonged to Buddy Holly . Another rumor had Richardson surviving the initial impact and crawling out of the aircraft in search for help , prompted by the fact that his body was found farther from the wreckage than the other three . Bass and his team took several X-rays of Richardson 's body and eventually concluded that the musician had indeed died instantly from extensive , nonsurvivable fractures to almost all of his bones ; no traces of lead were found from any bullet . Coroner Smiley 's original report was , therefore , confirmed . In March 2015 , the National Transportation Safety Board ( NTSB ) received a request to reopen the investigation into the accident . The request was made by L.J.","entities":["L.J.","Richardson","Jay Perry","William Bass","Smiley","National Transportation Safety Board",".22 (5.6 mm) pistol","original report","aircraft accident","request to reopen the investigation","The Day the Music Died","Buddy Holly"],"facts":["L.J. -PARTICIPATED_IN-> Super Bowl XXXIX","L.J. -MEMBER_OF-> Eagles","L.J. -MEMBER_OF-> Philadelphia","Richardson -LOCATED_IN-> Texas","Richardson -LOCATED_IN-> cornfield"]}
