It was released in August 1992 as the first of five singles from his third studio album , T-R-O-U-B-L-E . The song became Tritt 's tenth entry on the Billboard Hot Country Singles & Tracks ( now Hot Country Songs ) charts , where it peaked at number 5 . Contents ( hide ) 1 Content 2 Critical reception 3 Music video 4 Chart performance 5 References 6 External links Content ( edit ) `` Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man '' is a moderate up - tempo whose lyrics centralize on a theme of economic injustice towards blue collar workers . The instrumentation features various forms of percussion from Sam Bacco , including crotales , wobble board , spoons and a broom . Richard Bennett and Wendell Cox play guitar solos before the third verse , and Brooks & Dunn , T. Graham Brown , George Jones , Little Texas , Dana McVicker , Tanya Tucker and Porter Wagoner all sing background vocals on the final chorus .","entities":["Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man","Tritt","Billboard Hot Country Singles & Tracks","Sam Bacco","Richard Bennett","Wendell Cox","Brooks & Dunn","T. Graham Brown","George Jones","Little Texas","Dana McVicker","Tanya Tucker","Porter Wagoner","economic injustice","blue collar workers"],"facts":["Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man -RELATED_TO-> Chicago Tribune","Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man -RELATED_TO-> Jimmie Rodgers","Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man -RELATED_TO-> country","Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man -RELATED_TO-> MetroLyrics","Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man -RELATED_TO-> Billboard"]}
[E950840] {"title":"Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man","text":"Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man
Music video ( edit ) Jack Cole directed the song 's music video . In the book Country Music Culture : From Hard Times to Heaven , Curtis W. Ellison wrote that the song 's music video `` confronted a litany of personal oppressions attributed to government policy '' that coincided with Bill Clinton 's presidential campaign . Chart performance ( edit ) `` Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man '' spent 20 weeks on the Billboard Hot Country Singles & Tracks ( now Hot Country Songs ) charts in 1992 , peaking at number 5 . The song also reached number 10 on the Canadian RPM Country Tracks charts . Chart ( 1992 ) Peak position Canada Country Tracks ( RPM ) 10 US Hot Country Songs ( Billboard ) 5 References ( edit ) ^ Jump up to : T-R-O-U-B-L-E ( cassette insert ) . Travis Tritt . Warner Bros. Records . 1992 . 45048 . Jump up ^ Hurst , Jack ( 13 August 1992 ) . `` Satellite show Travis Tritt to launch new album from Park West '' . Chicago Tribune . Retrieved 28 August 2009 .","entities":["Bill Clinton","Chicago Tribune","Warner Bros. Records","Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man","Billboard Hot Country Singles & Tracks","Jack Cole","Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man music video","Country Music Culture: From Hard Times to Heaven","Curtis W. Ellison","government policy","Bill Clinton's presidential campaign","Canadian RPM Country Tracks","T-R-O-U-B-L-E","Satellite show Travis Tritt to launch new album from Park West","Jack Hurst"],"facts":["Bill Clinton -RELATED_TO-> executive privilege","Bill Clinton -RELATED_TO-> Lewinsky scandal","Bill Clinton -RELATED_TO-> Impeachment of Bill Clinton","Bill Clinton -RELATED_TO-> Most recent raise in salary","Bill Clinton -RELATED_TO-> Apology Resolution"]}
[E08086b] {"title":"Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man","text":"Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man
Critical reception ( edit ) Teresa M. Walker , in her review for the Gainesville Sun , said that with the assistance from superstars on the final chorus , the song `` should shoot up the charts . '' Dave Larsen of the Dayton Daily News cited it as one of the stronger tracks on the album , saying that the album `` works best when Tritt sticks with the populist approach . '' Alanna Nash of Entertainment Weekly said of the song , `` Tritt finds a solid image for his laborer 's lament ( ... ) But the melody is so slight that he resorts to an acoustic arrangement that evokes Jimmie Rodgers and the young Roy Acuff . '' Deborah Evans Price , of Billboard magazine gave the song a mixed review , saying that while the final chorus of song features famous country artists , it was too bad that the entire song is n't as strong as the last verse . Music video ( edit ) Jack Cole directed the song 's music video .","entities":["Entertainment Weekly","Billboard","Jimmie Rodgers","Roy Acuff","Lord Have Mercy on the Working Man","Teresa M. Walker","Gainesville Sun","Dave Larsen","Dayton Daily News","Alanna Nash","Deborah Evans Price","Jack Cole","music video"],"facts":["Entertainment Weekly -RELATED_TO-> Entertainment Weekly poll","Entertainment Weekly -RELATED_TO-> scene where Meredith is crying","Entertainment Weekly -RELATED_TO-> 'Grey's Anatomy' season finale recap: A Grey Goes Down","Entertainment Weekly -RELATED_TO-> Grey's Anatomy, Chyler Leigh, ... TV Season Finale Awards: YOUR 2012 Winners! Photo 9 of 20","Entertainment Weekly -RELATED_TO-> Tamara Tunie"]}
