Angular cheilitis - wikipedia Angular cheilitis Jump to : navigation , search Angular cheilitis Synonyms Rhagades , perlèche , cheilosis , angular cheilosis , commissural cheilitis , angular stomatitis Bilateral angular cheilitis in an elderly individual with false teeth , iron deficiency anemia and dry mouth Pronunciation / kaɪˈlaɪtɪs / Specialty Dermatology Symptoms Redness , skin breakdown and crusting at the corner of the mouth Usual onset Children , 30s to 60s Duration Days to years Causes Infection , irritation , allergies Treatment Based on cause , barrier cream Frequency 0.7 % of the population Angular cheilitis ( AC ) , is inflammation of one or both corners of the mouth . Often the corners are red with skin breakdown and crusting . It can also be itchy or painful . The condition can last for days to years . Angular cheilitis is a type of cheilitis ( inflammation of the lips ) . Angular cheilitis can be caused by infection , irritation , or allergies . Infections include by fungi such as Candida albicans and bacteria such as Staph .","entities":["irritation","infection","Allergies","Staph","Dermatology","Angular cheilitis","Candida albicans","barrier cream","Cheilitis"],"facts":["Pearl sac -RELATED_TO-> irritation","infection -RELATED_TO-> Fungi","infection -RELATED_TO-> bacterial viruses","infection -RELATED_TO-> bacterial species","infection -RELATED_TO-> Secondary opportunistic infection"]}
