The Wizard turns out to be a `` humbug '' and can only provide a placebo heart made of silk and filled with sawdust . However , this is enough to please the Tin Woodman , who , with or without a heart , was all along the most tender and emotional of Dorothy 's companions ( just as the Scarecrow was the wisest and the Cowardly Lion the bravest ) . When he accidentally crushes an insect , he is grief - stricken and , ironically , claims that he must be careful about such things , while those with hearts do not need such care . This tenderness remains with him throughout the series , as in The Patchwork Girl of Oz , where he refuses to let a butterfly be maimed for the casting of a spell . When Dorothy returns home to her farm in Kansas , the Tin Woodman returns to the Winkie Country to rule as emperor . Later , he has his subjects construct a palace made entirely of tin -- from the architecture all the way down to the flowers in the garden . Baum emphasized that the Tin Woodman remains alive , in contrast to the windup mechanical man Tik - Tok Dorothy meets in a later book .","entities":["Kansas","farm","Baum","Tin Woodman","Scarecrow","Cowardly Lion","Winkie Country","Wizard","placebo heart","The Patchwork Girl of Oz","butterfly","palace","Tik-Tok","insect","Dorothy"],"facts":["Kansas -LOCATED_IN-> U.S.","Abilene -LOCATED_IN-> Kansas","Lincoln -LOCATED_IN-> Kansas","Dodge City -LOCATED_IN-> Kansas","Kansas -LOCATED_IN-> United States"]}
