You can help by adding to it . ( February 2014 ) In The Religion of Science Fiction , Frederick A. Kreuziger explores the theory of history implied by Dick 's creation of the two alternative realities : Neither of the two worlds , however , the revised version of the outcome of WWII nor the fictional account of our present world , is anywhere near similar to the world we are familiar with . But they could be ! This is what the book is about . The book argues that this world , described twice , although differently each time , is exactly the world we know and are familiar with . Indeed , it is the only world we know : the world of chance , luck , fate . Avram Davidson praised the novel as a `` superior work of fiction '' , citing Dick 's use of the I Ching as `` fascinating '' . Davidson concluded that `` It 's all here -- extrapolation , suspense , action , art , philosophy , plot , ( and ) character . '' The Man in the High Castle secured for Dick the 1963 Hugo Award for Best Novel . A new paperback edition of the novel was published in 1992 by Vintage Books .","entities":["WWII","Dick","The Man in the High Castle","I Ching","The Religion of Science Fiction","Frederick A. Kreuziger","Avram Davidson","1963 Hugo Award for Best Novel","Vintage Books"],"facts":["Tatsuhei Mise -RELATED_TO-> WWII","portrait of Chiang Kai-shek -RELATED_TO-> WWII","New South Sea pearl projects commenced -RELATED_TO-> WWII","Israel -RELATED_TO-> WWII","Research on photosynthetic carbon metabolism -RELATED_TO-> WWII"]}
