Fact or fiction ( edit ) The degree to which the story is fiction has been disputed . In his biography of Orwell , George Orwell : A Life , Bernard Crick cast doubt on the idea that Orwell himself actually shot an elephant . No independent account of Orwell 's actions has been found and there was no official record of the incident , which was unusual considering the destruction of valuable property . Peter Davison , the editor of Orwell 's Complete Works , includes an interview with George Stuart , a contemporary of Orwell in Burma , who said that Orwell was transferred to Kathar as punishment for shooting an elephant . `` An elephant was considered a valuable asset to any timber firm ... and Orwell would have been severely reprimanded for such unnecessary slaughter . It was not long after the incident that he was transferred from Moulmein to a quiet post in Upper Burma called Katha . '' Davison also includes in the complete works a news item from the Rangoon Gazette , March 22 , 1926 which describes a Major E.C. Kenny shooting an elephant in similar circumstances .","entities":["Burma","Moulmein","George Orwell: A Life","Bernard Crick","Peter Davison","Complete Works","George Stuart","Upper Burma","Katha","shooting an elephant","Rangoon Gazette","Rangoon Gazette news item","Major E.C. Kenny","Major E.C. Kenny shooting an elephant","Shooting an Elephant"],"facts":["Myanmar Jeeps -LOCATED_IN-> Burma","Chin Dwin Star Jeeps -LOCATED_IN-> Burma","traditional new year -LOCATED_IN-> Burma","English narrator -LOCATED_IN-> Burma","New South Sea pearl projects commenced -LOCATED_IN-> Burma"]}
