Stand - your - ground law - wikipedia Stand - your - ground law Jump to : navigation , search `` Stand your ground '' redirects here . For other uses , see Stand Your Ground ( disambiguation ) . A stand - your - ground law ( sometimes called `` line in the sand '' or `` no duty to retreat '' law ) is a justification in a criminal case , whereby defendants can `` stand their ground '' and use force without retreating , in order to protect and defend themselves or others against threats or perceived threats . An example is where there is no duty to retreat from any place where they have a lawful right to be , and that they may use any level of force if they reasonably believe the threat rises to the level of being an imminent and immediate threat of serious bodily harm and or death . One case describes `` the ' stand your ground ' law ... a person has a right to expect absolute safety in a place they have a right to be , and may use deadly force to repel an unlawful intruder . ''","entities":["force","Threats","criminal case","Deadly force","Stand-your-ground law"],"facts":["force -RELATED_TO-> dark side of the Force","force -RELATED_TO-> light side of the Force","force -RELATED_TO-> state elections","force -RELATED_TO-> Magnet","force -RELATED_TO-> Presidential election"]}
[E5d2975] {"title":"Stand-your-ground law","text":"Stand-your-ground law
At English common law there is no duty to retreat before a person may use reasonable force against an attacker , nor need a person wait to be attacked before using such force , but one who chooses not to retreat , when retreat would be a safe and easy option , might find it harder to justify his use of force as ' reasonable ' . Any force used must be reasonable in the circumstances as the person honestly perceived them to be , after making allowance for the fact that some degree of excess force might still be reasonable in the heat of the moment . It was once thought that in ' householder defence ' cases , excessive force would be regarded as unreasonable only if `` grossly disproportionate '' , but that view has twice been rejected by the courts . Grossly disproportionate force can never be reasonable , but lesser force might or might not be , depending on the circumstances . In R v Ray ( 2017 ) the Lord Chief Justice , giving the judgment of the Court of Appeal , said : `` If there is a threat of confrontation in the street , then the option to retreat may be important in determining whether the use of any force was reasonable .","entities":["Court of Appeal","Lord Chief Justice","Excessive force","English common law","Duty to retreat","Stand-your-ground law","reasonable force","householder defence","grossly disproportionate force","R v Ray"],"facts":["Court of Appeal -RELATED_TO-> Court of Appeal decision","Court of Appeal -RELATED_TO-> House of Lords","Buwaneka Aluwihare -RELATED_TO-> Court of Appeal","Court of Session in Scotland -RELATED_TO-> Court of Appeal","High Court -RELATED_TO-> Court of Appeal"]}
