Historian Robert Middlekauff summarized scholarly research on the nature of Loyalist support as follows : The largest number of loyalists were found in the middle colonies : many tenant farmers of New York supported the king , for example , as did many of the Dutch in the colony and in New Jersey . The Germans in Pennsylvania tried to stay out of the Revolution , just as many Quakers did , and when that failed , clung to the familiar connection rather than embrace the new . Highland Scots in the Carolinas , a fair number of Anglican clergy and their parishioners in Connecticut and New York , a few Presbyterians in the southern colonies , and a large number of the Iroquois Indians stayed loyal to the king . Johnson Hall , seat of Sir John Johnson in the Mohawk Valley New York City and Long Island were the British military and political base of operations in North America from 1776 to 1783 and had a large concentration of Loyalists , many of whom were refugees from other states . According to Calhoon , Loyalists tended to be older and wealthier , but there were also many Loyalists of humble means .","entities":["Pennsylvania","New York City","New Jersey","New York","Connecticut","North America","Loyalists","Robert Middlekauff","middle colonies","tenant farmers of New York","the king","Dutch in the colony","Germans in Pennsylvania","Quakers","Highland Scots"],"facts":["Pennsylvania -LOCATED_IN-> US","Pennsylvania -LOCATED_IN-> East Coast of the United States","Reading -LOCATED_IN-> Pennsylvania","Sewickley -LOCATED_IN-> Pennsylvania","Pennsylvania -LOCATED_IN-> United States"]}
